\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Natural-orbital transformation for AIMs with 11, 13, 16, and 26 bath sites (rows, from top to bottom). The three columns show the successive steps of the transformation: (i) diagonalization of the bath density matrix followed by the bonding/antibonding rotation, (ii) tridiagonalization of the resulting empty and filled blocks, and (iii) the density matrix in the final natural-orbital basis with the groundstate calculated with DMRG. We write in caption the difference between the groundstate energies of the star and natural orbital representations. Again for the last, the impurity index is at site 0 and active site at respectively 7, 8, 9 and 14 (ignore the red lines).\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:four_by_three}{{6}{9}{Natural-orbital transformation for AIMs with 11, 13, 16, and 26 bath sites (rows, from top to bottom). The three columns show the successive steps of the transformation: (i) diagonalization of the bath density matrix followed by the bonding/antibonding rotation, (ii) tridiagonalization of the resulting empty and filled blocks, and (iii) the density matrix in the final natural-orbital basis with the groundstate calculated with DMRG. We write in caption the difference between the groundstate energies of the star and natural orbital representations. Again for the last, the impurity index is at site 0 and active site at respectively 7, 8, 9 and 14 (ignore the red lines).\relax }{figure.caption.6}{}}
